  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 152 PM CDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nThere are currently some scattered showers mainly over south central \nSD, and will continue to stick around into the evening. These rain \nshowers will most likely stay as rain, though a few rumbles of \nthunder cannot be ruled out during the afternoon. While south \ncentral SD is experiencing these showers, the rest of central and \nnortheastern SD and west central SD will continue to have some \nwildfire smoke aloft today and into Friday. Most of the smoke will \nstay aloft and create hazy skies. Though, some of it could get to \nthe surface to create some moderate air quality in some areas.  \n\nAn upper-level shortwave will be moving over SD Friday afternoon \nwhich will help to support a warm front moving through the state. \nThis front will interact will the moisture in the air as well as the \ninstability, with CAPE values around/above 1000 J/kg, to create some \nthunderstorms Friday afternoon into the evening. There is currently \na 20-40% chance for scattered storms and showers to develop over \nnorth central SD Friday afternoon into the evening. The lower \nconfidence/chances is coming from a decent cap the models have over \nnorth central SD that could keep storms from developing. Some high-\nres models keep from developing storms Friday due to the cap. Other \nmodels have storms developing in ND then moving into north central \nSD, and other have the storms overcoming the cap and develop in \nnorth central SD. As these storms move east into the evening, the \nmodels show them dissipating before getting into northeastern SD due \nto a much stronger cap over that area. If any storm is able to \novercome that cap to develop, there is a slight (level 2 of 5) to \nmarginal (level 1 of 5) risk it could become severe. Strong lapse \nrates of 7-8 C/km and wind shear of 30-40 kts will help to cause the \nprimary threat of hail up to 1 inch in diameter and damaging wind \ngust of 60 mph.  \n\nAn upper-level ridge will start to build over SD this weekend into \nthe beginning of next week. This will help warm air to push into \ncentral and northeastern SD. The warmest days looks to be Saturday \nand Sunday, with temperatures in the upper 90s to mid 100s. Dew \npoints will be in the mid 50s to low 60s over central SD and the mid \nto upper 60s over northeastern SD, which will give minimum relative \nhumidity values between 18-40%. These lower values will help to keep \nthe heat index values near the temperatures. Heat index values and \ntemperatures will have little recovery overnight Saturday into \nSunday, falling into the low 70s. This does keep the area out of \nextreme heat warning criteria, though it is right on the threshold. \nA heat headline might be needed and considered for the weekend \ndepending on how the next couple of model runs pan out.",
